ing list, reload and retain it when local saving succeeds.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"save-recovery\",\n+      \"path\": \"VISION.md\",\n+      \"pointer\": \"The household can explicitly retry saving the current session, including its needed choices.\"\n+    }\n+  ]\n+}\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/.gitignore\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\n+node_modules/\n+dist/\n+.cache/\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/.prettierignore\n@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@\n+VISION.md\n+.bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs\n+package-lock.json\n+node_modules/\n+dist/\n+.bstack/assessment.json\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/.prettierrc.json\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\n+{\n+  \"proseWrap\": \"preserve\"\n+}\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/AGENTS.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@\n+# Project guidance\n+\n+Read [VISION.md](VISION.md), [GLOSSARY.md](GLOSSARY.md) and [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md) before changing behaviour or module responsibilities. [README.md](README.md) owns setup and commands.\n+\n+Run `npm run check` for the maintained native checks and real browser journey. Preserve the Rice journey and save-failure/retry outcomes. Review documentation impact using `.bstack/project.json`; inspect every unmapped path. Use `.bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs` with an explicit base and reviewed assessment for portable maintenance evidence. This is local verification, not hosted CI or a release workflow.\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/DESIGN.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@\n+# Design\n+\n+## Static household application\n+\n+The browser owns the household session. There is no application backend, remote API, account or stock accounting. `src/main.ts` wires browser storage to the pantry session; `src/ui.ts` renders and handles controls. `src/pantry.ts` owns name reuse, needed choices and session persistence status behind a `Persistence` interface. `src/storage.ts` implements that interface and validates local data before accepting it. The domain has no browser dependency. UI uses the public pantry interface; storage details stay in the adapter and composition root.\n+\n+UI input -> pantry operation -> current session -> persistence adapter -> local storage. Rendering reads copied item values and save status. A storage exception never rolls back session edits. Explicit retry saves the whole current session and clears unsaved status only on success. A failed or invalid load shows a warning and keeps saving blocked until the household explicitly retries, so startup never silently overwrites unreadable data.\n+\n+## Acceptance\n+\n+Add Rice, mark it needed, view Rice on the shopping list, reload and retain Rice and its needed choice after successful saving. Trimmed case-insensitive duplicate names reuse the existing item without resetting needed; Brown Rice and White Rice remain distinct. Empty names are rejected. Needed can be toggled independently of stock. Save failure keeps editing and shopping-list use available, visibly marks unsaved changes, and explicit retry retains the latest session once saving succeeds. Invalid stored data is reported rather than accepted or silently overwritten.\n+\n+## Principles and review\n+\n+- Local ownership: VISION.md owns this commitment. Review application changes for remote calls, accounts and stock accounting. No exception in version one; a changed boundary needs author approval.\n+- Cohesive responsibilities: this design owns the model/adapter/UI split because purchase meaning must remain independent of browser storage failures. ESLint forbids domain imports and UI access to storage; review other dependency changes. Only relative static imports are supported; aliases, re-exports and dynamic imports need a reviewed rule expansion and new proof. No private-import exception.\n+- Honest saving: VISION.md owns the usable-session and retention commitments. Native behaviour tests and the real browser journey exercise storage failure and recovery. No exception to clearing unsaved status only after successful saving.\n+- Checked inputs: local data is untrusted. Strict TypeScript and runtime adapter validation enforce item shape, trimmed unique names and boolean needed values. No silent coercion or discarded invalid data.\n+- One maintained command: `npm run check` owns format, lint, type, build, behaviour and browser checks with fail-fast composition. Configurations own their values; no duplicated rule engine. Exceptions require a scoped reason and removal condition reviewed against the vision.\n+\n+A future change to purchase naming belongs in the model and its tests; a save-format change belongs in the adapter and its tests while preserving the Persistence contract. UI presentation changes should not require those owners to change. These are proposed locality benefits, not history measurements.\n+\n+## Maintenance\n+\n+The selected first maintenance contract is `.bstack/project.json` (foundation finding F-003). It maps implementation and tooling to the vision, glossary, design and guidance, with `npm run check` as its required native leaf. There are no generated documentation sections. Assess every mapped document and every unmapped path against the actual diff; a passing checker does not approve semantic claims. Commit implementation, collect a portable `.bstack/assessment.json` against an explicit ancestor, review and bind its fingerprint, then commit that assessment separately. The initial foundation uses an empty baseline commit, not an empty diff substituted for later work. Run `.bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs` in the working tree and in a different-root clean clone with isolated home and cache. The checker is copied byte-for-byte from the complete installed package and requires only Node and Git; declared native leaf tools are installed by `npm ci`. Local author review owns selection and semantic approval. No hosted delivery integration is selected.\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/GLOSSARY.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@\n+# Glossary\n+\n+- Item: one named pantry supply. Trimmed case-insensitive full names reuse the same item; different full supplied names distinguish variants.\n+- Needed: the household intention to buy an item, independent of stock.\n+- Shopping list: the items marked as needing to buy.\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@\n+# Pantry list\n+\n+A local household pantry list. Add a named supply and mark it needed to see it on the shopping list. There are no quantities, stock levels, accounts or remote services.\n+\n+## Setup and use\n+\n+Use Node 24 or later, npm, Git and installed Google Chrome. Run `npm ci`, then `npm run build` and `npm start`. Open http://127.0.0.1:4173. The loopback server serves static development files only; the application runs in the browser without a backend. Keep the same origin for retained data. Clearing site data removes the household list. File URLs are not the supported persistence origin. No external request is needed by the application.\n+\n+## Checks\n+\n+`npm run check` runs formatting, lint, strict types, build, public behaviour tests and the actual Chrome journey. `npm run journey` observes the Rice journey, duplicate names, named variants, save failure, continued editing and retry in a disposable browser context. It prints observations and browser versions. `npm run format` formats maintained code and configs; the approved vision's exact bytes are preserved. Tool versions and dependency resolution are pinned in package-lock.json. No CI is configured.\n+\n+## Maintenance verification\n+\n+Read [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md#maintenance) and `.bstack/project.json`. The selected installed dependency-free checker is `.bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs`. Given a reviewed committed `.bstack/assessment.json`, run `node .bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs --repo . --base BASE_COMMIT --assessment .bstack/assessment.json --json`. It invokes required native leaves afresh; install their pinned dev tools with `npm ci`. Run the same command in a clean clone at another root with isolated home/cache. Retain the returned checker-result.json and command captures locally. The assessment and semantic review remain explicit; no agent installation, hosted CI or delivery service is required by the resulting checkout.\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/VISION.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@\n+# Pantry list vision\n+\n+## Purpose and responsibility\n+Pantry list helps one household record named pantry supplies and see which supplies it needs to buy.\n+The project owns a clear path from recording an item to marking it needed and viewing it on the shopping list.\n+These commitments come from the author-approved brief and intent, not from project history.\n+\n+## Household simplicity\n+An item is one named pantry supply, and the shopping list contains items marked as needing to buy.\n+Needed records the household intention to buy independently of stock.\n+The household supplies the names that make sense to it.\n+Names are trimmed, and a case-insensitive full-name match reuses the existing item.\n+Variants remain distinct when their full supplied names differ, such as Brown Rice and White Rice.\n+Accept changes that make recording supplies and reviewing needed items easier to understand.\n+Resist changes that require quantities or stock levels to complete this journey.\n+\n+## Local control and honest persistence\n+Version one runs locally in a browser without an account, server or remote service.\n+The household can add Rice, mark it needed, view it on the shopping list, reload and retain it when local saving succeeds.\n+A failed local save keeps the current session usable and clearly exposes unsaved changes.\n+The household can explicitly retry saving the current session, including its needed choices.\n+The session remains editable while storage is unavailable, and unsaved status clears only after successful saving.\n+Reload retention is promised only after successful local saving.\n+Accept changes that preserve a usable household session and make the status of local saving understandable.\n+Resist changes that silently lose household choices or promise persistence without successful saving.\n+\n+## Boundaries\n+Quantities, stock levels, accounts, shared remote synchronization and hosted services are outside version one.\n+The project does not own deployment, release or delivery services.\n+\n+## Contribution tests\n+A change fits when it improves the household's named-item and needed-list journey while preserving local use and truthful saving.\n+Resist a change when it complicates that journey with inventory accounting or dependence on a remote system.\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/eslint.config.mjs\n@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@\n+import tseslint from \"typescript-eslint\";\n+\n+export default tseslint.config(\n+  { ignores: [\"dist/**\", \"node_modules/**\"] },\n+  ...tseslint.configs.recommended,\n+  {\n+    files: [\"src/pantry.ts\"],\n+    rules: {\n+      \"no-restricted-imports\": [\n+        \"error\",\n+        {\n+          patterns: [\n+            {\n+              group: [\"*\", \"**\"],\n+              message: \"Pantry domain must not import infrastructure.\",\n+            },\n+          ],\n+        },\n+      ],\n+      \"no-restricted-globals\": [\n+        \"error\",\n+        \"window\",\n+        \"document\",\n+        \"localStorage\",\n+        \"fetch\",\n+      ],\n+    },\n+  },\n+  {\n+    files: [\"src/ui.ts\"],\n+    rules: {\n+      \"no-restricted-imports\": [\n+        \"error\",\n+        {\n+          patterns: [\n+            {\n+              group: [\"*storage*\", \"**/storage*\"],\n+              message: \"UI must use the pantry public interface.\",\n+            },\n+          ],\n+        },\n+      ],\n+    },\n+  },\n+);\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/index.html\n@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@\n+<!doctype html>\n+<html lang=\"en\">\n+  <head>\n+    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n+    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n+    <title>Pantry list</title>\n+    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\" />\n+  </head>\n+  <body>\n+    <main id=\"app\"></main>\n+    <script type=\"module\" src=\"dist/main.js\"></script>\n+  </body>\n+</html>\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/package-lo